A1. Hard plodding and tight squeezing put this one on his feet. ~ CALLOUS
A7. A hard thing in the lives of newlyweds. ~ BISCUIT
A14. A pet name for friend husband. ~ BEAST
A15. Aint this awful? ~ FIERCE
A16. This will represent the Republican Party and also stand for Roosevelt's policies. ~ RP
A18. Be sure and make this one accurate. ~ TRUE
A19. It is against the law. ~ REBEL
A20. Turn this map around. ~ PAM
A22. By Jove! but this one has ripping teeth. ~ SAW
A23. Thanks, this to you. ~ SAME
A24. This is a great one but not exactly superior. ~ ERIE
A26. A season to do some fast work. ~ LENTEN
A31. A pet name for friend wife. ~ NAG
A32. Business for this pair is picking up. ~ TONGS
A34. This is how clevah an Englishman is. ~ DEUCED
A36. Part of the code. ~ NR
A37. What a steal magnate does for pleasure. ~ LOOTS
A39. All lit up. ~ ABLAZE
A41. This carries little weight with Gandhi. ~ SER
A42. What some of the big business men are now going into. ~ RETREAT
A44. "Me boy friend," sez ewe. ~ RAM
A46. The work of some chiseler. ~ STATUE
A48. Onion extract. ~ TEARS
A50. Listen for the musical note. ~ RE
A52. Few and far between. ~ SPARSE
A54. These can be made from a lot of scraps. ~ RIOTS
A56. The kind of a man a spinster likes. ~ ANY
A58. Look out! ~ SENTRY
A60. Can you beat this? ~ DRUM
A61. This will help to fill the bill. ~ ITEM
A63. Spat. ~ ROW
A65. A pen name. ~ STY
A66. These had to fight the blues. ~ GRAYS
A68. This leads to something deep. ~ ADIT
A70. It makes the young younger and old older. ~ ER
A71. One of Hawthorne's characters. ~ HESTER
A73. "A voice in the old village choir." ~ TENOR
A75. Be careful! there is a hitch to this. (plural) ~ TETHERS
A76. This may help solve your problems. ~ ALGEBRA

D1. In Washington this was put off the floor of the House and given a beating. ~ CARPET
D2. What she puts on during supper. (abbr.) ~ LB
D3. Grant, but not generally speaking. ~ LET
D4. This pair have pulled together for years. ~ OARS
D5. You will find this is customary in puzzles. ~ USUAL
D6. Spifflicated. ~ STEWED
D8. "Of all sad words—" ~ IF
D9. He has always been a dear to a stenog. ~ SIR
D10. Abie's follow-up letters. ~ CEES
D11. This does not refer to a hick town. ~ URBAN
D12. The forgotten man. ~ ICEMAN
D13. You should get this quickly, even if it is full of stops. ~ TELEGRAM
D17. This will give you a period before a sentence. ~ PAROLE
D21. Of lower rank than a major. (plur.) ~ MINORS
D25. Me, too. ~ EGO
D27. Best station to go to, when out of gas. ~ NEAREST
D28. This is a "pipe." ~ TUBE
D29. Splash and dash. ~ ECLAT
D30. If you wish this kind of a puzzle, do more thinking and less erasing. ~ NEATER
D33. Hold on to these if you wish to stand well. ~ STRAPS
D35. These two letters will get you going in circles. ~ DZ
D38. You may bristle at this but consult your dictionary. (noun plur.) ~ SETAE
D40. We make mistakes here too. ~ ERRORS
D41. What, no gingerale? ~ STRAIGHT
D43. This is not often left on busy street corners. ~ TURN
D45. We think this is clever; how about you? ~ ASTUTE
D47. Half wits. ~ TS
D49. You may need help for this one. ~ AID
D51. This follows the fish in another course. ~ ENTREE
D53. Pardon us for having so many mistakes in this puzzle. ~ ERRATA
D55. Let's talk Turkey and call it Izmir. ~ SMYRNA
D57. This has caused many uprisings. ~ YEAST
D59. A cracked-up voice. ~ YODEL
D62. A female moth. ~ MYTH
D64. This will help make things go up. ~ WING
D67. A kind of saw. ~ SEE
D69. Part of the corn belt. ~ TOE
D72. This leaves tracks crossing roads. ~ RR
D74. A superb ending for this puzzle. ~ RB


